<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.6. The wave equation continued</title>

  <p><alert>MTH 401</alert></p>
  <p>
    Let's put a few things together now.  We want to solve the equation with both specified boundary
    conditions AND initial conditions.  
  </p>
  <remark>
    <p>Words of wisdom:  It is 
  generally a good idea to start by identifying which are the boundary conditions and 
  which are the initial conditions, what is the length of the string, and
  also determine if any part of this, such as the equation with the corresponding boundary conditions, is something 
  we have previously solved...
  </p>
  </remark>


  <exercise workspace="4.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Solve the wave equation with fixed boundary conditions (the 
        ends of the string are clamped) and
  initial conditions where both initial position and initial velocity are non-zero. 
  <md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} \amp  =   4\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial
      x^2}</mrow>
<mrow>u(0,t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
<mrow>u(\pi,t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
<mrow>u(x,0) \amp  =   \pi x-x^2</mrow>
<mrow>\pd{u}{t}(x,0)  \amp  =  x</mrow>
</md></p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-6-soln">
      <p>
        So <m>L = \pi</m> and <m>v=2</m> and this is a wave equation with 
        fixed ends.  We already have the general solution from the last worksheet where now 
        we will simplify <m>\frac{n\pi v}{L} = nv</m> and <m>\frac{n\pi}{L}=n</m>:
        <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \cos\left(2nt\right)\sin\left(nx\right) +  B_n \sin\left(2nt\right)\sin\left(nx\right).
         </md>
         Now we need to apply the initial conditions.  
      </p>
      <p>
        Start with <m>u(x,0) = \pi x-x^2 = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n\sin(nx)+0</m>
        which is a Fourier Sine series with coefficients:
        <md>
          A_n=\frac2\pi \int_0^\pi (\pi x - x^2)\sin(nx)~dx= -\frac{4(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^3\pi}
        </md>
        
      </p>
      <p>
        Now the second initial condition needs <m>\pd u t</m>. 
        <md>
          \pd ut =\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} -2n A_n \sin\left(2nt\right)\sin\left(nx\right) +  2nB_n \cos\left(2nt\right)\sin\left(nx\right)
        
        </md>
        so then 
        <md>
          \pd ut(x,0) =x = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}  2nB_n \sin\left(nx\right)
        
        </md>
        This is a Fourier Sine series with coefficients <m>2nB_n</m> and not just <m>B_n</m>! So
        <md>
          2nB_n = \frac2\pi \int_0^\pi x \sin(nx)~dx = \frac{-2(-1)^n}{n}
        </md>
        So <m>{B_n = \frac{-(-1)^n}{n^2}}</m>.  Then the specific solution is 
        <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} -\frac{4(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^3\pi} \cos\left(2nt\right)\sin\left(nx\right) +  \frac{-(-1)^n}{n^2}\sin\left(2nt\right)\sin\left(nx\right).
         </md>

      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>


  <definition xml:id="def-free-ends">
    <statement>
        <p>When an end is <em>fixed</em> or <em>clamped</em> at the boundary <m>x=a</m>, 
          the boundary condition is <m>u(a,t)=0</m>. 
        We've seen this a lot. 
        </p> 
        <p>When the 
        end is <term>free</term> instead, we have a different boundary condition: 
        <md>\ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(a,t) = 0</md>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <exercise workspace="4in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the general solution to the following wave equation when the ends are <em>free</em> rather than 
  clamped. (Note no initial conditions are given so alas we can only find the general solution here.) 
  <md>
<mrow>\pdtwo{u}{t} \amp  =   4 \pdtwo{u}{x}</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(0,t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(L,t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
</md></p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-6-soln">
      <p>
        We already did this on the previous worksheet.  We could do it again from scratch if we want to.  Either way we'll get 
        <md>
          u(x,t) =A_0t + B_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \cos\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) +  B_n \sin\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="4in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let's go back to the first problem and make one small change. Instead of 
        clamped ends, we'll assume both ends are free. Though we'll keep the wave speed and initial 
        conditions the same.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is, solve the wave equation with the following boundary conditions and initial conditions:
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} \amp  =   4\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial
      x^2}</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(0,t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(\pi,t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
<mrow>u(x,0) \amp  =   \pi x-x^2</mrow>
<mrow>\pd{u}{t}(x,0)  \amp  =  x</mrow>
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-6-soln">
      <p>
        Take the general solution from the previous problem with <m>L =\pi</m> and <m>v=2</m>:
        <md>
          u(x,t) =A_0t + B_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \cos\left(2nt\right)\cos\left(nx\right) +  B_n \sin\left(2nt\right)\cos\left(nx\right)
        </md>
        The first initial condition:
        <md>
          u(x,0) = \pi x - x^2 = B_0 + \sum_{n=1}^\infty A_n\cos(nx)+0
        </md>
        (we usually see <m>B_0</m> called <m>A_0</m> but it's just a different name.)
        This is a Fourier Cosine Series with solutions
        <md>
                    <mrow>B_0 \amp = \frac1\pi \int_0^\pi(\pi x - x^2)~dx = \frac{\pi^2}{6} </mrow>
                    <mrow>A_n \amp = \frac2\pi\int_0^\pi(\pi x - x^2)\cos(nx)~dx = \frac{-2(1+(-1)^n)}{n^2} </mrow>
        </md>
      
      </p>
      <p>
        The second initial condition relies on <m>\pd ut</m> where 
        <md>
          \pd ut = A_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} -2n A_n \sin\left(2nt\right)\cos\left(nx\right) +  2nB_n \cos\left(2nt\right)\cos\left(nx\right)
        </md>
        so then plugging in 
        <md>
          \pd ut(x,0) x = = A_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} 2nB_n \cos\left(2nt\right)\cos\left(nx\right)
        </md>
        And we have the Fourier Cosine series with solutions:
        <md>
                    <mrow>A_0 \amp = \frac1\pi \int_0^\pi(x)~dx = \frac{\pi}{2} </mrow>
                    <mrow>2nB_n \amp = \frac2\pi\int_0^\pi(x)\cos(nx)~dx = \frac{2(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^2 \pi} </mrow>
        </md>
        so <m>{B_n = \frac{-1+(-1)^n}{n^3\pi}}</m>.  All together now:

        <md>
          u(x,t) =\frac{\pi}{2}t + \frac{\pi^2}{6} + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{-2(1+(-1)^n)}{n^2}\cos\left(2nt\right)\cos\left(nx\right) +  \frac{-1+(-1)^n}{n^3\pi} \sin\left(2nt\right)\cos\left(nx\right)
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
